00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:58.860
into your business.

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:01.980
But first of all, let's
think about how the world is

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:03.750
organized and how the
world is organized

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:05.790
from a geographic perspective.

00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:09.210
Any location on the planet, be
it in the middle of the desert

00:02:09.210 --> 00:02:11.820
or the middle of the
ocean, is a location.

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:13.290
And that location
can be measured

00:02:13.290 --> 00:02:15.660
by a set of coordinates,
the most common of which

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:17.460
are latitude and longitude.

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:19.470
And latitude and
longitude coordinates

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:22.050
can be sensed
typically by a GPS.

00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:24.420
But as I'll be talking
about in this session,

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:26.040
if you don't have
access to a GPS,

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:28.770
you can also sense them in
new and innovative ways,

00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:35.270
such as using cellular towers,
using beacons, and using Wi-Fi.

00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:37.020
And we'll talk a little
bit about the APIs

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:38.480
that are available for that.

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:39.855
But then beyond
just the location

00:02:39.855 --> 00:02:41.740
that has a latitude
and a longitude,

00:02:41.740 --> 00:02:43.740
particularly when
civilization comes to a place,

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:45.250
you have addresses.

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:47.160
And in Maps, we
have an Address API

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:48.930
and we have a set of
tools for addresses

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:51.450
so that you can get
your addresses right.

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:54.960
So autocomplete makes for a
really nice user experience.

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:57.000
Think about if you're
building an app.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:59.590
You're trying to type an
address on a tiny little phone

00:02:59.590 --> 00:03:01.346
or you're trying to
do it on the web, that

00:03:01.346 --> 00:03:02.970
addresses that are
very, very different

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:04.480
in different countries.

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:06.480
[? Ankar ?] and I have a
session later on today.

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:08.890
We'll be going into a
lot more detail on that.

00:03:08.890 --> 00:03:11.370
But some of the things that
are available in the Geo

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:12.270
APIs to make--

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:14.490
if you're building
applications to make managing

00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:16.860
addresses a lot more easy.

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:19.210
Then on top of that,
you've got places.

00:03:19.210 --> 00:03:21.090
So a place is like
a point of interest.

00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:24.060
And at Google, we have
over 100 million places

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:25.260
stored in a database.

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:28.020
And there is a unique
identifier for those places,

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:30.780
which then allows you to
get metadata for that place.

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:33.270
So, for example, each floor
in this convention center

00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:34.680
is a place.

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:37.100
And each of those
has a primary key

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:41.280
called the Place ID that
allows you to access metadata

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:44.970
about that place, for example,
opening hours, photos,

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:45.790
things like that.

00:03:45.790 --> 00:03:49.320
If the place is a restaurant,
you could get a menu.

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:50.310
You could get photos.

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:52.770
You could get reviews that
people have done when they've

00:03:52.770 --> 00:03:54.630
gone into that restaurant.

00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:59.040
And [AUDIO OUT] Oh,
has my mic gone out?

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:01.590
With the Places API
as well, you also

00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:04.200
get the ability to detect
the place that you're in.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:05.880
And this is more
than just looking up

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:07.394
a latitude and
longitude and saying

00:04:07.394 --> 00:04:09.310
that you're in a particular
point of interest.

00:04:09.310 --> 00:04:11.310
It has a lot of
intelligence behind it.

00:04:11.310 --> 00:04:12.907
So, for example, the
scenario I always

00:04:12.907 --> 00:04:14.490
like to think about
is if I'm standing

00:04:14.490 --> 00:04:16.073
in the middle of a
street, and there's

00:04:16.073 --> 00:04:18.720
a bus stop 5 meters that way,
and there's a coffee shop

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:21.190
5 meters that way, where am I?

00:04:21.190 --> 00:04:23.580
Am I at the bus stop, or
am I at the coffee shop?

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:25.850
Well, what if the coffee
shop closes at 2:00 PM,

00:04:25.850 --> 00:04:27.317
and I'm there at 3:00 PM?

00:04:27.317 --> 00:04:29.150
Then it's not likely
I'm in the coffee shop.

00:04:29.150 --> 00:04:30.900
It's more likely
I'm at the bus stop.

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:32.790
That kind of intelligence
with all the places

00:04:32.790 --> 00:04:34.662
is made available to
you as a developer.

00:04:34.662 --> 00:04:36.120
And with that kind
of intelligence,

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:37.661
you can start building
and empowering

00:04:37.661 --> 00:04:39.127
a lot of new scenarios.

00:04:39.127 --> 00:04:40.710
And at 4 o'clock
today, we'll be going

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:42.330
into a lot more detail on that.

00:04:42.330 --> 00:04:44.260
There's a session
called [AUDIO OUT]

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:45.790
location intelligence.

00:04:45.790 --> 00:04:47.770
And we'll be going into
that in a lot of detail

00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:50.064
there and having a
bit of fun with it.

00:04:50.064 --> 00:04:51.730
But I'm going to
focus, in this session,

00:04:51.730 --> 00:04:55.000
on geolocation and how
you can do geolocation.

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:57.610
Now, why would you
want to geolocate?

00:04:57.610 --> 00:04:59.480
Now, there's a number
of reasons why.

00:04:59.480 --> 00:05:03.160
Number one, it's most common
to think in terms of GPS,

00:05:03.160 --> 00:05:04.960
but what if GPS isn't available?

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:06.700
There are a number of reasons
why it may not be available.

00:05:06.700 --> 00:05:08.283
Like if you're in a
big city and there

00:05:08.283 --> 00:05:10.030
are a lot of skyscrapers
in that city,

00:05:10.030 --> 00:05:12.550
you may not get a clear line
of sight to the satellites

00:05:12.550 --> 00:05:15.430
to be able to do GPS location.

00:05:15.430 --> 00:05:19.450
Or as sensors for Wi-Fi and
for a cell tower-- like I say,

00:05:19.450 --> 00:05:21.570
for something this
small can be built--

00:05:21.570 --> 00:05:24.492
get cheaper and
cheaper, and GPS--

00:05:24.492 --> 00:05:26.950
unfortunately, they're a larger
circuit and more expensive,

00:05:26.950 --> 00:05:30.310
both in battery and cost
to build that, you now

00:05:30.310 --> 00:05:32.520
have new options where
you can determine

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:33.820
a location of something.

00:05:33.820 --> 00:05:35.770
So I can put something
like this on my pet.

00:05:35.770 --> 00:05:37.969
And then from my pet, I
can determine his location

00:05:37.969 --> 00:05:40.510
if he ever gets lost because it
would be heartbreaking if you

00:05:40.510 --> 00:05:42.820
ever lose your little doggy.

00:05:42.820 --> 00:05:45.940
So these are some of the reasons
why you'd want to geolocate.

00:05:45.940 --> 00:05:48.640
And when you think about
asset management enterprises,

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:52.550
the asset management industry
is growing to about a $5 billion

00:05:52.550 --> 00:05:55.181
industry in the next five years.

00:05:55.181 --> 00:05:57.180
And how do we do asset
management at the moment?

00:05:57.180 --> 00:05:59.390
It's typically we put a
bar code on every device,

00:05:59.390 --> 00:06:00.973
and we scan it in,
and we scan it out.

00:06:00.973 --> 00:06:03.550
But we don't actually know
where the device is located.

00:06:03.550 --> 00:06:06.040
So cheap circuits,
this guy's built

00:06:06.040 --> 00:06:09.460
on a first-generation
Raspberry Pi, which was great

00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:13.060
because [AUDIO OUT] But the
newest Raspberry Pi, one just

00:06:13.060 --> 00:06:15.910
came out last week which is
the size of a stick of gum.

00:06:15.910 --> 00:06:18.160
And it has a built-in Wi-Fi
circuit, and it costs $10.

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:20.367
I actually ordered one
so that I could do it

00:06:20.367 --> 00:06:22.450
for this conference, but
it didn't arrive on time,

00:06:22.450 --> 00:06:24.370
so I'm stuck with the
older generation, one

00:06:24.370 --> 00:06:29.340
that's [AUDIO OUT]
has access to Wi-Fi.

00:06:29.340 --> 00:06:31.860
So then you can
scan Wi-Fi signals,

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:34.170
and you can geolocate off
of those Wi-Fi signals.

00:06:34.170 --> 00:06:36.300
And the cost of that
is very, very low.

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:37.629
You plug a battery into him.

00:06:37.629 --> 00:06:39.420
And we all have those
battery packs that we

00:06:39.420 --> 00:06:40.964
can use to reboost our phones.

00:06:40.964 --> 00:06:42.630
And a device like
that could potentially

00:06:42.630 --> 00:06:46.000
run for a week off
of a simple battery.

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:49.510
So the Google Maps
Geolocation API [AUDIO OUT]

00:06:49.510 --> 00:06:53.440
offer to [AUDIO OUT]
for devices where

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:56.380
if you want to scan cell
circuits, if you want to scan

00:06:56.380 --> 00:06:59.290
Wi-Fi, and you want to get back
a latitude and a longitude.

00:06:59.290 --> 00:07:01.690
So we'll take a look
about how it works.

00:07:01.690 --> 00:07:04.480
Like I said, it uses
Wi-Fi and cell signals.

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:08.050
So the Raspberry Pi in
this guy runs Linux.

00:07:08.050 --> 00:07:10.279
And if you're using
Linux, there's a command.

00:07:10.279 --> 00:07:12.070
There's a shall command
that you can call--

00:07:12.070 --> 00:07:14.500
sudo wlan0 dev scan--

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:16.070
something like that.

00:07:16.070 --> 00:07:17.755
What you can do, like
in Linux, there's

00:07:17.755 --> 00:07:20.300
a simple command where
you can scan for Wi-Fi.

00:07:20.300 --> 00:07:22.600
And if you have like a
cell circuit in there,

00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:24.970
there's an API that you
can use to scan for cell.

00:07:24.970 --> 00:07:27.670
And what that will do is
it'll return a big text block.

00:07:27.670 --> 00:07:30.880
And that big text block has a
list of all the Wi-Fi towers

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:31.400
that it--

00:07:31.400 --> 00:07:34.030
sorry, all the Wi-Fi access
points that it can see.

00:07:34.030 --> 00:07:36.640
And every Wi-Fi access point
has a unique identifier.

00:07:36.640 --> 00:07:39.550
You're probably familiar with a
MAC address and a network card.

00:07:39.550 --> 00:07:41.500
An access point has a
very similar address.

00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:43.470
It's sometimes called a BSSID.

00:07:43.470 --> 00:07:45.490
But usually, BSSID
and MAC address

00:07:45.490 --> 00:07:47.150
are used interchangeably.

00:07:47.150 --> 00:07:50.922
So you can get the BSSIDs of the
Wi-Fi access points around you.

00:07:50.922 --> 00:07:52.630
And usually when you
do a scan like that,

00:07:52.630 --> 00:07:54.742
you get a signal
strength back as well.

00:07:54.742 --> 00:07:56.950
You've probably noticed like
when you open your phone

00:07:56.950 --> 00:07:59.050
and you're looking for Wi-Fi
or you open your laptop

00:07:59.050 --> 00:08:00.610
and you're looking
for Wi-Fi, there's

00:08:00.610 --> 00:08:03.070
a little visualization of the
signal strength, usually one

00:08:03.070 --> 00:08:05.000
bar, two bars or three bars.

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:06.520
And that signal
strength actually

00:08:06.520 --> 00:08:08.800
comes back as a decibel loss.

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:11.350
And if you know the decibel
loss of the primary signal,

00:08:11.350 --> 00:08:13.330
you can start looking
at how you can

00:08:13.330 --> 00:08:16.420
calculate the distance between
you and that access points.

00:08:16.420 --> 00:08:18.340
And then once you
have all of this data,

00:08:18.340 --> 00:08:21.670
then you can call Google and
you can call the Geolocation API

00:08:21.670 --> 00:08:24.505
with a simple HTTP call
that you pass it the data,

00:08:24.505 --> 00:08:26.380
and I'll show an example
of that in a moment.

00:08:26.380 --> 00:08:27.490
But you pass it the data.

00:08:27.490 --> 00:08:29.230
You pass it your API key.

00:08:29.230 --> 00:08:32.740
And it will return back some
location info and some accuracy

00:08:32.740 --> 00:08:33.699
estimation around that.

00:08:33.699 --> 00:08:36.031
It's not going to be able to
pinpoint you to the nearest

00:08:36.031 --> 00:08:37.600
millimeter, but
what it will do is

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:40.390
it will give you a rough
estimate for, say, I think

00:08:40.390 --> 00:08:42.210
you're at this
latitude and longitude,

00:08:42.210 --> 00:08:43.659
and you're within
20 meters of it,

00:08:43.659 --> 00:08:46.230
or something along those lines.

00:08:46.230 --> 00:08:47.550
So it works like this.

00:08:47.550 --> 00:08:50.890
So say you have your
device, and what it does

00:08:50.890 --> 00:08:54.140
is it scans, like I said,
each of the cell towers

00:08:54.140 --> 00:08:56.480
or each of the Wi-Fi
access points around you.

00:08:56.480 --> 00:08:58.700
And it figures out
a distance to them

00:08:58.700 --> 00:09:00.910
based on the signal strength.

00:09:00.910 --> 00:09:03.630
And that can be plugged
into a formula like this.

00:09:03.630 --> 00:09:07.350
So this is the freespace
path loss formula.

00:09:07.350 --> 00:09:10.630
So what it is is
it's a decibel value.

00:09:10.630 --> 00:09:14.090
And you know the decibel
value upon scanning,

00:09:14.090 --> 00:09:15.520
because it will
tell you the loss.

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:17.811
So it will be something like
negative-- a really strong

00:09:17.811 --> 00:09:20.390
signal is about
negative 30 decibels.

00:09:20.390 --> 00:09:22.072
A not great signal
is about minus 60.

00:09:22.072 --> 00:09:23.780
If you're below minus
60, you're probably

00:09:23.780 --> 00:09:25.342
going to be dropping
off a Wi-Fi.

00:09:25.342 --> 00:09:27.050
If you're above minus
30, you're probably

00:09:27.050 --> 00:09:28.930
sitting on top of
the access point.

00:09:28.930 --> 00:09:32.450
So a good, strong signal is
going to be between negative 30

00:09:32.450 --> 00:09:34.550
and negative 60 decibels loss.

00:09:34.550 --> 00:09:35.750
So you know that value.

00:09:35.750 --> 00:09:37.550
You know that decibel value.

00:09:37.550 --> 00:09:40.700
And you know the frequency,
which is F in this formula,

00:09:40.700 --> 00:09:42.740
because your Wi-Fi
access point's going

00:09:42.740 --> 00:09:44.630
to be operating
on 2.4 gigahertz,

00:09:44.630 --> 00:09:46.910
or it's going to be
operating on 5 gigahertz.

00:09:46.910 --> 00:09:50.004
And you know K. K is a constant.

00:09:50.004 --> 00:09:51.920
And there are a number
of different constants,

00:09:51.920 --> 00:09:54.530
but it will give you
32.44 as the constant

00:09:54.530 --> 00:09:57.020
you use if you measure
D in kilometers

00:09:57.020 --> 00:09:59.510
and if you measure
F in megahertz.

00:09:59.510 --> 00:10:02.527
If you change F to gigahertz
or change D to meters,

00:10:02.527 --> 00:10:03.860
then that constant would change.

00:10:03.860 --> 00:10:05.984
But there's a lookup table
where you can find them.

00:10:05.984 --> 00:10:09.800
So in this case, I've been
using 32.44 as the constant.

00:10:09.800 --> 00:10:13.370
So I have K. I have
F, and I have dB.

00:10:13.370 --> 00:10:14.930
So that will allow
me to calculate

00:10:14.930 --> 00:10:18.290
D where D is the distance
between me and that access

00:10:18.290 --> 00:10:19.020
point.

00:10:19.020 --> 00:10:20.436
Now, what's the
drawback for that?

00:10:20.436 --> 00:10:23.190
Anybody know?

00:10:23.190 --> 00:10:24.180
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:10:24.180 --> 00:10:25.320
LAURENCE MORONEY: Yeah.

00:10:25.320 --> 00:10:29.085
I mean, I know I'm 20 meters
from the access point,

00:10:29.085 --> 00:10:33.180
but that's like I'm on a circle
with a radius of 20 meters,

00:10:33.180 --> 00:10:34.210
and I could be anywhere.

00:10:34.210 --> 00:10:36.196
So how do you think you
could get around that?

00:10:36.196 --> 00:10:37.170
AUDIENCE: Triangulate.

00:10:37.170 --> 00:10:38.160
LAURENCE MORONEY:
Triangulate with a number

00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:39.780
of different ones, absolutely.

00:10:39.780 --> 00:10:41.730
So let's take a look at
how that would happen.

00:10:41.730 --> 00:10:43.099
So here I am with my device.

00:10:43.099 --> 00:10:44.640
And it's probably
a little inaccurate

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:46.710
because I'm using a cell
phone that probably has a GPS,

00:10:46.710 --> 00:10:47.340
but you'll forgive me.

00:10:47.340 --> 00:10:49.050
It's the best
graphic I could find.

00:10:49.050 --> 00:10:50.970
So here I am with my device.

00:10:50.970 --> 00:10:54.630
And I do a scan, and I see
these three access points.

00:10:54.630 --> 00:10:56.100
So from these three
access points,

00:10:56.100 --> 00:10:59.640
I can establish a radius that I
am from the first access point.

00:10:59.640 --> 00:11:02.370
I can establish a radius that
I am from the second access

00:11:02.370 --> 00:11:03.060
point.

00:11:03.060 --> 00:11:05.640
And I can establish a radius
that I am from the third access

00:11:05.640 --> 00:11:06.600
point.

00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:09.090
Now, the overlap of
these three then gives me

00:11:09.090 --> 00:11:12.330
a pretty good estimate for where
I am at this moment in time.

00:11:12.330 --> 00:11:14.010
Now, this is nice
because if you're

00:11:14.010 --> 00:11:17.020
in a location where there
was a lot of access points,

00:11:17.020 --> 00:11:20.370
then the area of that overlap
will get smaller and smaller

00:11:20.370 --> 00:11:21.310
and smaller.

00:11:21.310 --> 00:11:23.220
And as a result, you've
got a better estimate

00:11:23.220 --> 00:11:24.825
for your actual location.

00:11:24.825 --> 00:11:28.130
And now, there's a few
drawbacks with this.

00:11:28.130 --> 00:11:31.080
The eagle-eyed amongst you,
if I go back to the formula,

00:11:31.080 --> 00:11:33.210
might have noticed that
it's an FSPL, which

00:11:33.210 --> 00:11:35.400
is the FreeSpace Path Loss.

00:11:35.400 --> 00:11:38.850
Now, there's rarely free space
between us and access points.

00:11:38.850 --> 00:11:41.370
So, for example there,
a 2.4 gigahertz signal,

00:11:41.370 --> 00:11:43.950
you know what the biggest
absorber of 2.4 gigahertz

00:11:43.950 --> 00:11:46.024
signals are to knock off
your signal strength?

00:11:46.024 --> 00:11:46.690
AUDIENCE: Water.

00:11:46.690 --> 00:11:48.550
LAURENCE MORONEY:
Water, excellent.

00:11:48.550 --> 00:11:49.850
Yeah, it's actually water.

00:11:49.850 --> 00:11:52.410
And what are you mostly made of?

00:11:52.410 --> 00:11:53.120
Right.

00:11:53.120 --> 00:11:54.769
So my protip, as
a Googler to you,

00:11:54.769 --> 00:11:56.560
is if you have a weak
Wi-Fi signal at home,

00:11:56.560 --> 00:11:59.150
ask your family to
lie down on the floor.

00:11:59.150 --> 00:12:01.300
And so they'll stop
blocking it and then

00:12:01.300 --> 00:12:02.590
you can get great Wi-Fi.

00:12:02.590 --> 00:12:04.930
But yeah, a 2.4 gigahertz
access point, water.

00:12:04.930 --> 00:12:07.800
So trees also are
made mostly of water

00:12:07.800 --> 00:12:09.770
so that that can knock
off the strength of it.

00:12:09.770 --> 00:12:11.830
So again, the more
of these access

00:12:11.830 --> 00:12:13.730
points you can scan, the better.

00:12:13.730 --> 00:12:16.420
And as a result, you
can greatly, greatly

00:12:16.420 --> 00:12:20.220
reduce the size of
the red circle here.

00:12:20.220 --> 00:12:23.830
OK, so now once you've done
that, this is the theory.

00:12:23.830 --> 00:12:25.990
And how does it actually
all work in practice?

00:12:25.990 --> 00:12:27.850
So if I'm building
an application

00:12:27.850 --> 00:12:31.270
and I scan the access points
near me, then all I have to do

00:12:31.270 --> 00:12:34.450
is build a JSON bundle
like this one with a list

00:12:34.450 --> 00:12:36.490
of my Wi-Fi access points.

00:12:36.490 --> 00:12:39.640
I list the MAC address of the
access point that I've scanned,

00:12:39.640 --> 00:12:41.550
and I list the signal
strength of that.

00:12:41.550 --> 00:12:44.260
And like I said, the commands,
like in Linux or in Android

00:12:44.260 --> 00:12:45.970
or on Windows or
on Mac, when you

00:12:45.970 --> 00:12:47.890
issue those commands
to scan Wi-Fi,

00:12:47.890 --> 00:12:49.420
they'll give you back this data.

00:12:49.420 --> 00:12:51.700
You also list the
signal-to-noise ratio.

00:12:51.700 --> 00:12:53.560
You sometimes get that;
you sometimes don't.

00:12:53.560 --> 00:12:55.972
If you don't get that, just
fill it in with a zero.

00:12:55.972 --> 00:12:57.430
And then you take
this and then you

00:12:57.430 --> 00:12:59.660
call the Google API with that.

00:12:59.660 --> 00:13:03.070
And here's just an example of
how you do it using a curl.

00:13:03.070 --> 00:13:03.870
So you curl.

00:13:03.870 --> 00:13:07.000
You give it-- in this case,
I'd store those values

00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:08.530
into a JSON file.

00:13:08.530 --> 00:13:11.860
And then you pass that with
the content-type set to JSON up

00:13:11.860 --> 00:13:15.880
to googleapis.com/g
eolocation/v1/geolocate,

00:13:15.880 --> 00:13:17.584
and then you give
it your API key.

00:13:17.584 --> 00:13:19.750
And if you've ever done any
Google Maps development,

00:13:19.750 --> 00:13:21.499
you'll see there's an
online console where

00:13:21.499 --> 00:13:22.960
you can go to get an API key.

00:13:22.960 --> 00:13:24.850
And just the API
key is what we use

00:13:24.850 --> 00:13:27.220
to know that it's
you that's calling it

00:13:27.220 --> 00:13:29.080
and not somebody
else using your key.

00:13:29.080 --> 00:13:31.286
And in many cases, some
services are billed,

00:13:31.286 --> 00:13:33.410
and you don't want other
people using your service.

00:13:33.410 --> 00:13:35.080
So you have an API key for that.

00:13:35.080 --> 00:13:38.200
And you just do this
call using the API key,

00:13:38.200 --> 00:13:40.250
and you'll get back
something like this.

00:13:40.250 --> 00:13:43.480
So in this case, I got back
a latitude and a longitude

00:13:43.480 --> 00:13:44.830
and an accuracy value.

00:13:44.830 --> 00:13:47.000
And the accuracy
value is in meters.

00:13:47.000 --> 00:13:49.510
So when I did this call based
on these two access points,

00:13:49.510 --> 00:13:51.190
these were well-known
access points.

00:13:51.190 --> 00:13:53.057
I had a nice signal off of them.

00:13:53.057 --> 00:13:54.640
And I was able to
get back an accuracy

00:13:54.640 --> 00:13:58.525
that it said I was somewhere
about 20 meters from that spot.

00:13:58.525 --> 00:14:00.400
So I'm going to do a
little demo, which means

00:14:00.400 --> 00:14:01.660
I need to start up the robot.

00:14:01.660 --> 00:14:05.409
And he takes a few minutes to
boot up, so just one second.

00:14:05.409 --> 00:14:06.950
So he takes a few
minutes to boot up.

00:14:06.950 --> 00:14:09.000
So he's got a Linux
operating system onboard,

00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:10.100
and that needs to boot up.

00:14:10.100 --> 00:14:12.820
And then I use VNC
to shell into him.

00:14:12.820 --> 00:14:14.900
Whoa, he's getting a bit fussy.

00:14:14.900 --> 00:14:16.310
I use VNC to shell into him.

00:14:16.310 --> 00:14:19.659
So while that's
all booting up, I'm

00:14:19.659 --> 00:14:21.450
just going to show a
little bit of the code

00:14:21.450 --> 00:14:22.380
that he actually uses.

00:14:26.810 --> 00:14:28.850
So here he is when I was
building him on my desk

00:14:28.850 --> 00:14:30.382
at home with his little friends.

00:14:30.382 --> 00:14:31.590
Anybody recognize his friend?

00:14:31.590 --> 00:14:33.410
Yeah.

00:14:33.410 --> 00:14:37.100
So on him, because
he runs Linux,

00:14:37.100 --> 00:14:39.590
I use the programming
language called Python.

00:14:39.590 --> 00:14:41.722
And I don't-- anybody
familiar with Python?

00:14:41.722 --> 00:14:42.670
Yeah, a lot of hands.

00:14:42.670 --> 00:14:43.360
Good.

00:14:43.360 --> 00:14:46.450
And Python is a really
fun, interesting language

00:14:46.450 --> 00:14:48.820
for doing something
like this because it's

00:14:48.820 --> 00:14:52.180
a very low-powered
computer onboard with him.

00:14:52.180 --> 00:14:54.790
So running an IDE or
stack kind of thing

00:14:54.790 --> 00:14:55.840
would be very, very slow.

00:14:55.840 --> 00:14:57.940
But if I build it in
Python, I can literally

00:14:57.940 --> 00:15:00.370
shell into him from my laptop.

00:15:00.370 --> 00:15:02.260
I can change code on the fly.

00:15:02.260 --> 00:15:03.430
I don't need to compile it--

00:15:03.430 --> 00:15:05.710
I know Python does kind
of sorter compile--

00:15:05.710 --> 00:15:07.990
and then just do some
edits, and off I go.

00:15:07.990 --> 00:15:11.020
So in this case, the command at
the top that you can see here--

00:15:11.020 --> 00:15:15.460
sudo iw dev wlan0 scan--

00:15:15.460 --> 00:15:18.340
is the Linux command that you
use to scan your Wi-Fi access

00:15:18.340 --> 00:15:19.139
points.

00:15:19.139 --> 00:15:20.680
So at any moment in
time-- and you'll

00:15:20.680 --> 00:15:23.020
see what the robot will do
is he will drive around.

00:15:23.020 --> 00:15:25.769
And whenever he hits a barrier,
he moves to avoid that barrier.

00:15:25.769 --> 00:15:28.060
But at the point that he's
moving to avoid the barrier,

00:15:28.060 --> 00:15:29.754
then he does a geolocation call.

00:15:29.754 --> 00:15:31.420
And what he's doing
in that case is he's

00:15:31.420 --> 00:15:33.280
issuing this command to Linux.

00:15:33.280 --> 00:15:35.320
He's getting the
values back from Linux.

00:15:35.320 --> 00:15:37.370
And it's a big
text blob from it.

00:15:37.370 --> 00:15:39.940
And then it's just as simple
as using regular expressions.

00:15:39.940 --> 00:15:41.773
I never thought I would
say that expression,

00:15:41.773 --> 00:15:44.980
but it's just as simple as
using a regular expression

00:15:44.980 --> 00:15:46.690
to scan for the BSS.

00:15:46.690 --> 00:15:49.030
So if you have a Linux box
and you issue that command,

00:15:49.030 --> 00:15:54.160
you'll see, in this big bunch
of text, all of the Wi-Fi access

00:15:54.160 --> 00:15:56.794
points near you,
including their BSS.

00:15:56.794 --> 00:15:58.210
And this is the
regular expression

00:15:58.210 --> 00:16:01.420
that you can use to just pluck
that string for the BSS out

00:16:01.420 --> 00:16:02.550
of the text blob.

00:16:02.550 --> 00:16:04.280
And then all I do
is I just turn--

00:16:04.280 --> 00:16:06.610
I create a list of all
my BSSes, and that's

00:16:06.610 --> 00:16:07.982
the third line of code.

00:16:07.982 --> 00:16:09.940
And then for the signal
and the signal strength

00:16:09.940 --> 00:16:11.898
that I mentioned, it's
really important for you

00:16:11.898 --> 00:16:14.650
to be able to pass the
signal strength up to Google.

00:16:14.650 --> 00:16:17.800
So then that was plugged
into the FSPL formula

00:16:17.800 --> 00:16:20.650
that we saw earlier on so
you can get back a location.

00:16:20.650 --> 00:16:23.560
The signal strength does
come as part of the return

00:16:23.560 --> 00:16:24.780
from a Linux call.

00:16:24.780 --> 00:16:26.980
And I just used a regular
expression for that,

00:16:26.980 --> 00:16:28.600
create a list out of them.

00:16:28.600 --> 00:16:30.100
And now that I have
these two lists,

00:16:30.100 --> 00:16:33.970
I can create that JSON
blob that I spoke about.

00:16:33.970 --> 00:16:37.120
So when I have that JSON blob,
I can just create a data item

00:16:37.120 --> 00:16:38.020
using Python.

00:16:38.020 --> 00:16:40.630
And then using the
requests library in Python,

00:16:40.630 --> 00:16:44.740
I can post up to the Geolocation
API, pass my API key,

00:16:44.740 --> 00:16:45.850
and get a value back.

00:16:45.850 --> 00:16:48.550
And then that value is going
to give me my geolocation.

00:16:48.550 --> 00:16:51.670
All right, this guy should
be booted up by now,

00:16:51.670 --> 00:16:52.820
so let me sign into him.

00:16:57.970 --> 00:17:00.160
And I'm going to put him
on the stage over here.

00:17:00.160 --> 00:17:02.410
Now you see why the stools
were laid there, so that he

00:17:02.410 --> 00:17:03.630
doesn't drive off the edge.

00:17:06.930 --> 00:17:08.520
So I'm just opening
up a terminal

00:17:08.520 --> 00:17:12.555
so that I can sign into him
and I can get him running.

00:17:12.555 --> 00:17:14.180
I've got to put the
mic down for a sec.

00:17:33.780 --> 00:17:34.980
And there he goes.

00:17:34.980 --> 00:17:35.959
So he starts driving--

00:17:35.959 --> 00:17:37.500
oh, he's trying to
go off the stage--

00:17:37.500 --> 00:17:38.730
until he sees a barrier.

00:17:38.730 --> 00:17:41.430
And then when he sees a barrier,
he'll take a look around.

00:17:41.430 --> 00:17:43.120
He does a geolocation
to say, hey,

00:17:43.120 --> 00:17:44.860
there was something
interesting here.

00:17:44.860 --> 00:17:47.469
So he can do an internal
mapping of where he is.

00:17:47.469 --> 00:17:49.260
So at that point, when
he's looking around,

00:17:49.260 --> 00:17:50.490
looking left and right--

00:17:50.490 --> 00:17:54.330
he doesn't have a backup camera,
so he might back into things.

00:17:54.330 --> 00:17:57.960
So when he looks left and
right like that, at that point,

00:17:57.960 --> 00:17:59.520
he's making the
call up to Google,

00:17:59.520 --> 00:18:01.740
giving the scan of
where he is right now,

00:18:01.740 --> 00:18:03.990
the Wi-Fi access points nearby.

00:18:03.990 --> 00:18:07.530
And I can do this, and
he gets a little sassy.

00:18:07.530 --> 00:18:09.960
Doing a scan of like
where he is right now,

00:18:09.960 --> 00:18:12.755
getting a location back, and
then writing it to Firebase.

00:18:12.755 --> 00:18:14.130
I'm going to put
him on the floor

00:18:14.130 --> 00:18:17.129
down here, let him
drive around for a bit.

00:18:17.129 --> 00:18:19.170
And then he would write
his location to Firebase.

00:18:19.170 --> 00:18:21.450
And then once it's
in Firebase, now I

00:18:21.450 --> 00:18:24.630
can start reading that
data out of Firebase

00:18:24.630 --> 00:18:26.940
and plot a map of
where he's been.

00:18:26.940 --> 00:18:29.046
Now, one small
drawback in demoing him

00:18:29.046 --> 00:18:30.420
in a place like
this, can anybody

00:18:30.420 --> 00:18:32.360
think of what it might be?

00:18:32.360 --> 00:18:35.720
Sometimes he gets a little
upset and he goes in circles.

00:18:35.720 --> 00:18:39.050
Anybody, why do you think I
would get a really bad location

00:18:39.050 --> 00:18:40.100
on him being here?

00:18:40.100 --> 00:18:40.964
AUDIENCE: Wi-Fi.

00:18:40.964 --> 00:18:43.380
LAURENCE MORONEY: Well, the
Wi-Fi is actually really good,

00:18:43.380 --> 00:18:44.694
but it's also temporary.

00:18:44.694 --> 00:18:46.110
These are temporary
hotspots, Next

00:18:46.110 --> 00:18:49.590
'17 has just set up for
these last few days.

00:18:49.590 --> 00:18:52.870
So he gets a pretty bad
signal because of that.

00:18:52.870 --> 00:18:54.180
So what I did--

00:18:54.180 --> 00:18:57.410
and I'll show next--

00:18:57.410 --> 00:19:01.960
is I took him in a cab, and
we drove around San Francisco.

00:19:01.960 --> 00:19:07.700
And so I did that location
check with him in San Francisco.

00:19:07.700 --> 00:19:09.780
And here's some of the
spots that he was actually

00:19:09.780 --> 00:19:12.250
able to geolocate himself to.

00:19:12.250 --> 00:19:14.940
So if you know San
Francisco pretty well,

00:19:14.940 --> 00:19:17.210
you'll see we, right
now, are here--

00:19:17.210 --> 00:19:18.840
[INAUDIBLE]

00:19:18.840 --> 00:19:21.870
So last night, I had
him scan some locations.

00:19:21.870 --> 00:19:23.280
I did not go up this street.

00:19:23.280 --> 00:19:25.140
But for some
reason, the accuracy

00:19:25.140 --> 00:19:26.250
didn't show the greatest.

00:19:26.250 --> 00:19:28.100
It showed me within
a certain distance.

00:19:28.100 --> 00:19:30.600
But I've been spending a lot
of time going up and down here,

00:19:30.600 --> 00:19:33.060
and I'm staying in a
hotel over here somewhere.

00:19:33.060 --> 00:19:36.330
So by putting him in
a car, in this case,

00:19:36.330 --> 00:19:40.626
and having him periodically call
the Geolocation API by sniffing

00:19:40.626 --> 00:19:42.750
signals around him, by
sniffing, in this case, just

00:19:42.750 --> 00:19:45.780
Wi-Fi signals around him, I was
able to get a pretty accurate

00:19:45.780 --> 00:19:46.920
lock on where he is.

00:19:46.920 --> 00:19:48.850
Pretty cool, right?

00:19:48.850 --> 00:19:52.100
Now, what can you start
doing with this kind of data?

00:19:52.100 --> 00:19:55.060
You can start turning it
into real intelligence.

00:19:55.060 --> 00:19:57.900
In this case, all I did
was I plotted it on a map.

00:19:57.900 --> 00:20:01.450
That's kind of nice, but
what if it was a much more

00:20:01.450 --> 00:20:03.490
expensive, much
more important asset

00:20:03.490 --> 00:20:04.722
than a simple little robot?

00:20:04.722 --> 00:20:05.680
What if this was like--

00:20:05.680 --> 00:20:08.290
I don't know-- a
$100,000 truck or a truck

00:20:08.290 --> 00:20:10.360
carrying a priceless cargo?

00:20:10.360 --> 00:20:12.967
Now what if I want to
geolocate that kind of thing?

00:20:12.967 --> 00:20:14.800
I could do that, and I
can plot it on a map,

00:20:14.800 --> 00:20:16.732
or I could timecode it.

00:20:16.732 --> 00:20:18.940
In this case here, you just
saw I put a bunch of pins

00:20:18.940 --> 00:20:20.040
on the map.

00:20:20.040 --> 00:20:21.666
But what if I time-coded them?

00:20:21.666 --> 00:20:24.040
Then you could actually tell
the route that I was taking.

00:20:24.040 --> 00:20:24.873
Was I heading north?

00:20:24.873 --> 00:20:25.790
Was I heading south?

00:20:25.790 --> 00:20:27.520
Where was I in this location?

00:20:27.520 --> 00:20:28.570
That kind of thing.

00:20:28.570 --> 00:20:31.120
So if it's a really expensive,
really valuable cargo,

00:20:31.120 --> 00:20:36.780
I can start doing that kind
of thing by getting a geocode,

00:20:36.780 --> 00:20:38.269
getting a lock on
his environment.

00:20:38.269 --> 00:20:40.560
And then when I write it to
a database, when I write it

00:20:40.560 --> 00:20:43.620
to something like Firebase, it's
really, really simple to do so.

00:20:43.620 --> 00:20:45.580
I could also write
that with a time code.

00:20:45.580 --> 00:20:47.121
So at some point
when I read it back,

00:20:47.121 --> 00:20:48.310
it gets pretty interesting.

00:20:48.310 --> 00:20:50.880
Now what if I have
1,000 of these,

00:20:50.880 --> 00:20:52.950
and they're spread
all over the world?

00:20:52.950 --> 00:20:55.740
Now I can start really
turning data about how they're

00:20:55.740 --> 00:20:57.750
being used into intelligence.

00:20:57.750 --> 00:20:59.880
Maybe there are some
cities in the world where

00:20:59.880 --> 00:21:02.120
I need to have trucks
where I don't have them,

00:21:02.120 --> 00:21:03.870
and there's some cities
in the world where

00:21:03.870 --> 00:21:05.157
I've got too many trucks.

00:21:05.157 --> 00:21:06.990
And I can see the routes
that they're taking

00:21:06.990 --> 00:21:10.237
and how they're being used and
things like that, so really,

00:21:10.237 --> 00:21:12.070
really start turning
data into intelligence.

00:21:12.070 --> 00:21:15.570
And there's one partner of
ours that they have this really

00:21:15.570 --> 00:21:16.519
innovative solution.

00:21:16.519 --> 00:21:18.810
And I'll show a little bit
more about them in a moment.

00:21:18.810 --> 00:21:22.680
But what they do is they put
a sensor into garbage cans.

00:21:22.680 --> 00:21:25.590
And here in the US-- like, I
live up in the Seattle area.

00:21:25.590 --> 00:21:28.470
And for me, my trash is
collected every Friday morning.

00:21:28.470 --> 00:21:30.930
Now, it doesn't matter
if my bin is empty

00:21:30.930 --> 00:21:32.700
or if my bin is overflowing.

00:21:32.700 --> 00:21:34.920
It's still collected
every Friday morning.

00:21:34.920 --> 00:21:36.960
And for example,
during the summer

00:21:36.960 --> 00:21:39.566
when it's hot, if the bins
have been filled on Tuesday

00:21:39.566 --> 00:21:41.190
and they're not picked
up until Friday,

00:21:41.190 --> 00:21:43.020
they can get a
little bit stinky.

00:21:43.020 --> 00:21:45.540
So what this company has done
is that they have sensors

00:21:45.540 --> 00:21:47.730
that they put into
bins, and these sensors,

00:21:47.730 --> 00:21:49.770
when they fill up,
when the bin is full,

00:21:49.770 --> 00:21:51.420
it recognizes that it's full.

00:21:51.420 --> 00:21:54.250
And the sensor
dials home and says,

00:21:54.250 --> 00:21:57.120
I'm a bin at this
location, and I'm full.

00:21:57.120 --> 00:21:59.550
So the company, instead
of having a set of trucks

00:21:59.550 --> 00:22:03.120
that have to go on fixed routes
in these areas on Monday,

00:22:03.120 --> 00:22:05.190
these areas on Tuesday,
what they can do

00:22:05.190 --> 00:22:09.060
is, on demand, they can see
the optimum routes to divert

00:22:09.060 --> 00:22:12.990
a truck to pick up trash
today so the trash is always

00:22:12.990 --> 00:22:15.210
picked up at the time
that the bin is filled.

00:22:15.210 --> 00:22:17.799
That's really clever, and
that's really, really cool.

00:22:17.799 --> 00:22:20.340
And who would have thought that
being able to put geolocation

00:22:20.340 --> 00:22:21.887
in a trash can
could end up being

00:22:21.887 --> 00:22:23.970
something that could make
life a little bit better

00:22:23.970 --> 00:22:24.700
for everybody?

00:22:24.700 --> 00:22:28.587
And I think that situations
like that and innovations

00:22:28.587 --> 00:22:30.670
like that are things that
are really, really cool.

00:22:30.670 --> 00:22:32.250
And when you start broadening
your mind about what

00:22:32.250 --> 00:22:34.234
you can do when you
can geolocate an asset,

00:22:34.234 --> 00:22:36.150
you can start coming up
with things like that.

00:22:36.150 --> 00:22:37.566
It's not just about
tracking pets.

00:22:37.566 --> 00:22:40.020
It's not just about
tracking trucks.

00:22:40.020 --> 00:22:42.719
There are 1,000 great solutions
that nobody's thought of yet.

00:22:42.719 --> 00:22:44.760
And hopefully, the technology
that we're building

00:22:44.760 --> 00:22:47.240
will help you to do that.

00:22:47.240 --> 00:22:50.480
So going further, there's
a lot of APIs around geo

00:22:50.480 --> 00:22:53.434
that also make scenarios
like this possible.

00:22:53.434 --> 00:22:55.100
So you've got the
location of something.

00:22:55.100 --> 00:22:55.910
Well, so what?

00:22:55.910 --> 00:22:58.610
How'd you get there?

00:22:58.610 --> 00:23:01.880
So there's a Distance Matrix
API, and there is a Roads API,

00:23:01.880 --> 00:23:04.764
and there's the Geocoding
API and other ones here

00:23:04.764 --> 00:23:06.680
that will allow you, for
example, to determine

00:23:06.680 --> 00:23:08.900
if I'm at location
A and I need to get

00:23:08.900 --> 00:23:11.810
to location B, what's the
optimal way of getting there?

00:23:11.810 --> 00:23:14.300
If I'm going right now,
it might be a lot faster

00:23:14.300 --> 00:23:15.356
to go on the highway.

00:23:15.356 --> 00:23:16.730
But if I'm going
at rush hour, it

00:23:16.730 --> 00:23:19.020
might be a lot slower to go
on the highway, scenarios

00:23:19.020 --> 00:23:19.940
such as that one.

00:23:19.940 --> 00:23:22.400
It doesn't have to be
the situation right now.

00:23:22.400 --> 00:23:24.794
You can actually pick a
situation in the future.

00:23:24.794 --> 00:23:26.960
And we've put a lot of
machine learning intelligence

00:23:26.960 --> 00:23:30.350
into understanding
distance and directions so

00:23:30.350 --> 00:23:33.590
that you can calculate optimum
times to get from A to B.

00:23:33.590 --> 00:23:35.900
There's the Places API that
I spoke about earlier on.

00:23:35.900 --> 00:23:37.100
So I don't know.

00:23:37.100 --> 00:23:40.610
Maybe in a scenario like
the missing pet one,

00:23:40.610 --> 00:23:42.979
you might realize the missing
pet is in the a store,

00:23:42.979 --> 00:23:44.270
or something along those lines.

00:23:44.270 --> 00:23:44.670
I don't know.

00:23:44.670 --> 00:23:46.211
And hopefully, he's
not being resold.

00:23:49.600 --> 00:23:52.990
And one of my personal favorites
is there is a Roads API.

00:23:52.990 --> 00:23:54.470
And I think this
one goes really,

00:23:54.470 --> 00:23:58.296
really nicely with the Wi-Fi
cell-based geolocation.

00:23:58.296 --> 00:23:59.670
Because as I told
you earlier on,

00:23:59.670 --> 00:24:01.450
there's an element
of uncertainty.

00:24:01.450 --> 00:24:03.820
You get back a latitude
and a longitude

00:24:03.820 --> 00:24:06.040
and you get a radius
where you might be.

00:24:06.040 --> 00:24:07.910
But if you are
tracking an asset,

00:24:07.910 --> 00:24:10.720
for example, a cargo
that's on a truck,

00:24:10.720 --> 00:24:12.880
you know that's going
to be driving on a road.

00:24:12.880 --> 00:24:16.180
So if the center of that
circle isn't on a road,

00:24:16.180 --> 00:24:17.770
with the Roads API,
you can actually

00:24:17.770 --> 00:24:20.080
snap to an actual road.

00:24:20.080 --> 00:24:23.350
And here's an example of,
like, this is near my house.

00:24:23.350 --> 00:24:26.740
And I took Freddy out
for a drive last week.

00:24:26.740 --> 00:24:28.870
And if you notice
the black line,

00:24:28.870 --> 00:24:31.660
the end points of the line
segments of the black line,

00:24:31.660 --> 00:24:36.490
like here and here and up here,
are where geolocation returned

00:24:36.490 --> 00:24:38.620
where it thought Freddy was.

00:24:38.620 --> 00:24:41.500
Now, I wasn't thankfully driving
in my neighbor's backyard

00:24:41.500 --> 00:24:42.780
at this point.

00:24:42.780 --> 00:24:46.060
And so what I did was I got all
of those points that were being

00:24:46.060 --> 00:24:47.980
plotted, that were
being returned

00:24:47.980 --> 00:24:50.350
to me by the Geolocation
API, and I said, look,

00:24:50.350 --> 00:24:52.090
I know I'm driving on a road.

00:24:52.090 --> 00:24:54.220
Let me pass that
to the Roads API.

00:24:54.220 --> 00:24:56.674
And then the Roads API
gave me back the blue line.

00:24:56.674 --> 00:24:58.090
And as you can
see, the blue line,

00:24:58.090 --> 00:25:00.340
it snapped perfectly
to the actual roads

00:25:00.340 --> 00:25:02.230
around my neighborhood,
which I thought

00:25:02.230 --> 00:25:03.290
was really, really cool.

00:25:03.290 --> 00:25:05.260
But in this case, I
didn't time code anything.

00:25:05.260 --> 00:25:07.877
So you don't know if I went
that way and around and up,

00:25:07.877 --> 00:25:10.210
or if I went that way, turned,
and went around this way.

00:25:10.210 --> 00:25:12.130
So by time-coding
points, you could

00:25:12.130 --> 00:25:14.140
make it even more
intelligent, but here,

00:25:14.140 --> 00:25:15.580
just being able
to snap to roads.

00:25:15.580 --> 00:25:19.210
So instead of just me having fun
driving around my neighborhood,

00:25:19.210 --> 00:25:22.990
again, if you have expensive
assets being shipped somewhere,

00:25:22.990 --> 00:25:25.840
and these are being geolocated
off of cheap circuits,

00:25:25.840 --> 00:25:28.180
you can then start doing
something like this to snap

00:25:28.180 --> 00:25:30.730
to roads to get intelligence
about where they are right now,

00:25:30.730 --> 00:25:34.340
where they're going,
and things like that.

00:25:34.340 --> 00:25:36.470
So then going beyond
this, then thinking

00:25:36.470 --> 00:25:37.970
we're here at a
cloud conference,

00:25:37.970 --> 00:25:40.460
and there's lots of
great tools for cloud.

00:25:40.460 --> 00:25:43.310
And three of these, the first
of them is Cloud Datalab.

00:25:43.310 --> 00:25:46.490
So Cloud Datalab is all
about taking in big data

00:25:46.490 --> 00:25:48.590
and turning it into
visualizations.

00:25:48.590 --> 00:25:51.390
So it's a suite of tools that
will allow you to do that.

00:25:51.390 --> 00:25:54.020
So again, if you have
many thousands of sensors

00:25:54.020 --> 00:25:56.480
around the world gathering
geolocation data,

00:25:56.480 --> 00:25:58.550
you've got big data.

00:25:58.550 --> 00:26:00.530
How do you want to slice
and dice that data?

00:26:00.530 --> 00:26:02.659
How do you want to turn
that into visualization?

00:26:02.659 --> 00:26:04.700
And the Cloud Datalab,
the idea behind that is it

00:26:04.700 --> 00:26:06.630
gives you the tools for that.

00:26:06.630 --> 00:26:09.260
And then there's the Cloud
Machine Learning APIs.

00:26:09.260 --> 00:26:11.300
So again, if you've
got big data and you

00:26:11.300 --> 00:26:13.370
want to identify
patterns in that data,

00:26:13.370 --> 00:26:15.890
for example, the trash truck
scenario that I was talking

00:26:15.890 --> 00:26:18.680
about, you can start
beginning to understand

00:26:18.680 --> 00:26:20.389
by training a model to say--

00:26:20.389 --> 00:26:20.930
I don't know.

00:26:20.930 --> 00:26:22.712
For example, trash
cans in this town

00:26:22.712 --> 00:26:24.920
tend to fill up quicker than
trash cans in that town.

00:26:24.920 --> 00:26:26.900
Or they fill up quicker
during the summer

00:26:26.900 --> 00:26:28.340
than they do in the winter.

00:26:28.340 --> 00:26:30.007
Or in the post-Christmas
period, they

00:26:30.007 --> 00:26:32.090
tend to fill up really
quickly because everybody's

00:26:32.090 --> 00:26:34.920
putting the wrapping paper and
the boxes and things like that.

00:26:34.920 --> 00:26:37.610
So those kind of patterns can
emerge from machine learning.

00:26:37.610 --> 00:26:39.650
And again, you can start
training your systems

00:26:39.650 --> 00:26:42.770
to be far more intelligent,
training them off of geodata

00:26:42.770 --> 00:26:45.290
and being able to
build better apps

00:26:45.290 --> 00:26:48.800
and build better scenarios, and
maybe build better businesses.

00:26:48.800 --> 00:26:50.610
And then the third
one is the Vision API.

00:26:50.610 --> 00:26:52.318
And if you were at
the keynote yesterday,

00:26:52.318 --> 00:26:55.940
the Vision API is now able to
detect things in video as well

00:26:55.940 --> 00:26:57.170
as in photographs.

00:26:57.170 --> 00:26:59.440
It can detect
sentiment in video.

00:26:59.440 --> 00:27:00.860
And it doesn't show here.

00:27:00.860 --> 00:27:02.360
It's hiding behind
Freddy's bow tie,

00:27:02.360 --> 00:27:04.550
but he's actually got a
little camera on his front.

00:27:04.550 --> 00:27:06.870
And this is like a $10 camera.

00:27:06.870 --> 00:27:09.950
And it's 1080p.

00:27:09.950 --> 00:27:13.490
So a device like that on a
simple computer with a camera

00:27:13.490 --> 00:27:16.100
on, it you could
start recording video.

00:27:16.100 --> 00:27:18.050
You can get a geocode location.

00:27:18.050 --> 00:27:20.450
You can tag where
that video was filmed.

00:27:20.450 --> 00:27:22.250
And then using things
like the Vision API,

00:27:22.250 --> 00:27:24.410
you can then even determine
what's in that video

00:27:24.410 --> 00:27:25.520
when you're filming it.

00:27:25.520 --> 00:27:29.180
Again, lots of great scenarios
to build great businesses on.

00:27:29.180 --> 00:27:31.700
And I'm hoping that--
today, I was talking

00:27:31.700 --> 00:27:34.259
about the company
that picked up trash,

00:27:34.259 --> 00:27:36.800
and that's Enevo and had this
innovative thing for picking up

00:27:36.800 --> 00:27:37.370
trash.

00:27:37.370 --> 00:27:39.650
What will I be talking
about next year?

00:27:39.650 --> 00:27:43.160
Maybe some people here,
listening to these talks,

00:27:43.160 --> 00:27:44.582
seeing what's
possible with cloud,

00:27:44.582 --> 00:27:46.040
and coming up with
great scenarios.

00:27:46.040 --> 00:27:47.870
Maybe even somebody in
this room today or somebody

00:27:47.870 --> 00:27:49.661
who was at the keynote
today and yesterday.

00:27:49.661 --> 00:27:52.220
And I'd love to hear about
how you're using these APIs

00:27:52.220 --> 00:27:54.360
and how you're
building on these APIs

00:27:54.360 --> 00:27:57.290
to really, really build
innovative scenarios.

00:27:57.290 --> 00:28:00.080
And the CTO of Enevo
gave us this quote,

00:28:00.080 --> 00:28:02.570
just really talking about
as the world's population is

00:28:02.570 --> 00:28:05.350
increasing and more
waste is being generated,

00:28:05.350 --> 00:28:07.310
then it's really
vital to make sure

00:28:07.310 --> 00:28:10.500
that waste collection is
as efficient as possible.

00:28:10.500 --> 00:28:12.030
And how more
efficient can you get

00:28:12.030 --> 00:28:14.390
than actually waste
collection on demand as

00:28:14.390 --> 00:28:16.700
opposed to the far
more primitive way

00:28:16.700 --> 00:28:20.200
that we have of your trash is
picked up on a particular day?

00:28:20.200 --> 00:28:21.580
Then there's the company Pod.

00:28:21.580 --> 00:28:23.900
And those are the guys that
make this little device

00:28:23.900 --> 00:28:25.330
that I spoke about.

00:28:25.330 --> 00:28:28.250
And this device, it
ha a Bluetooth sensor.

00:28:28.250 --> 00:28:29.960
It has Wi-Fi.

00:28:29.960 --> 00:28:31.940
And it has a 2G cell signal.

00:28:31.940 --> 00:28:35.420
So if it's on the pet's collar
and the pet goes missing,

00:28:35.420 --> 00:28:39.670
once you ping it, it will dial
home over the 2G cell signal.

00:28:39.670 --> 00:28:41.570
And it will also
use the 2G circuit

00:28:41.570 --> 00:28:44.690
to sniff towers around
it, sniff Wi-Fi around it.

00:28:44.690 --> 00:28:47.180
It will use the BLE to
scan beacons around it.

00:28:47.180 --> 00:28:49.709
And it will determine a location
from that and dial home.

00:28:49.709 --> 00:28:51.250
And then it will
show up on your app.

00:28:51.250 --> 00:28:53.450
And I just got
this the other day.

00:28:53.450 --> 00:28:55.760
So my dog isn't with me, but
I've been playing with it.

00:28:55.760 --> 00:28:57.593
And if you want to see
some of the locations

00:28:57.593 --> 00:29:00.746
that it detected me as being in
because I have it in my pocket,

00:29:00.746 --> 00:29:02.120
I can show you on
my phone later.

00:29:02.120 --> 00:29:03.210
It's pretty cool.

00:29:03.210 --> 00:29:06.800
And again, I've had a dog
now for nearly 10 years.

00:29:06.800 --> 00:29:10.104
And I used to go to
RadioShack and the Apple store

00:29:10.104 --> 00:29:12.020
and other places like
that all the time to see

00:29:12.020 --> 00:29:13.646
if I could get a
locator for my dog

00:29:13.646 --> 00:29:15.020
because I don't
want to lose him,

00:29:15.020 --> 00:29:17.990
but they were always like almost
the size of a water bottle.

00:29:17.990 --> 00:29:19.730
Now, because of this
kind of technology,

00:29:19.730 --> 00:29:22.810
they could fit in
something as big as this.

00:29:22.810 --> 00:29:25.090
And then the last one
is Doctor on Demand.

00:29:25.090 --> 00:29:27.340
And again, using geolocation,
what Doctor on Demand

00:29:27.340 --> 00:29:30.700
have done, very, very simple,
but very, very innovative,

00:29:30.700 --> 00:29:33.550
is that we all have
primary doctors or GPs.

00:29:33.550 --> 00:29:37.076
But how often is it that we
need to talk to our doctor,

00:29:37.076 --> 00:29:38.950
but we have to make an
appointment with them?

00:29:38.950 --> 00:29:40.991
It's a little bit like
the trash pickup scenario,

00:29:40.991 --> 00:29:42.790
that we have to go
according to a schedule.

00:29:42.790 --> 00:29:44.710
We have to be able to
fit into something.

00:29:44.710 --> 00:29:46.660
But if we need a
doctor on demand,

00:29:46.660 --> 00:29:48.220
and there are a
bunch of doctors who

00:29:48.220 --> 00:29:50.709
don't have appointments
at this moment in time,

00:29:50.709 --> 00:29:52.000
we need to know where they are.

00:29:52.000 --> 00:29:53.500
They need to know where we are.

00:29:53.500 --> 00:29:56.380
So using geolocation, this
company, Doctor on Demand,

00:29:56.380 --> 00:29:59.596
have been able to match
customers with doctors so

00:29:59.596 --> 00:30:00.970
that they can be
seen by a doctor

00:30:00.970 --> 00:30:02.860
far sooner than if
they had to wait

00:30:02.860 --> 00:30:05.200
on an appointment for
a specific doctor.

00:30:05.200 --> 00:30:07.540
And particularly in large
health care organizations

00:30:07.540 --> 00:30:10.000
where there's a centralized
database of your health care,

00:30:10.000 --> 00:30:11.500
it's not like this
doctor is going

00:30:11.500 --> 00:30:14.207
to be totally unfamiliar
with your medical record.

00:30:14.207 --> 00:30:15.790
You can actually go
see a doctor who's

00:30:15.790 --> 00:30:17.530
very familiar with
the medical record,

00:30:17.530 --> 00:30:19.780
and you can see them
sooner rather than later.

00:30:19.780 --> 00:30:22.352
So again, geolocation opened
up a scenario like this.

00:30:22.352 --> 00:30:24.310
And this kind of thing
probably wasn't possible

00:30:24.310 --> 00:30:26.334
even three or four,
five years ago.

00:30:26.334 --> 00:30:28.000
And the senior software
engineer of that

00:30:28.000 --> 00:30:31.000
was saying, a direct
connection between maps,

00:30:31.000 --> 00:30:34.220
between geolocation, and
improving patient care.

00:30:34.220 --> 00:30:37.630
Now, what better reason
to be able to work

00:30:37.630 --> 00:30:38.790
on technology like this--

00:30:38.790 --> 00:30:42.290
It inspires me every day; I love
working on this technology--

00:30:42.290 --> 00:30:44.350
than to be able to
read a quote like that,

00:30:44.350 --> 00:30:46.150
a direct connection
between what you do

00:30:46.150 --> 00:30:49.570
and improving care for people,
improving people's lives.

00:30:49.570 --> 00:30:51.020
And that's what I'm all about.

00:30:51.020 --> 00:30:52.870
And that's what we're trying
to be all about with Google,

00:30:52.870 --> 00:30:54.700
trying to be all
about here in Cloud--

00:30:54.700 --> 00:30:57.760
improve quality of your life,
give you tools, give you

00:30:57.760 --> 00:31:01.210
frameworks, give you APIs that
you can build something that

00:31:01.210 --> 00:31:02.836
will improve other
people's lives,

00:31:02.836 --> 00:31:04.210
whether it be
tracking their pet,

00:31:04.210 --> 00:31:05.560
whether it be picking
up their trash,

00:31:05.560 --> 00:31:07.412
whether it be connecting
them with doctors.

00:31:07.412 --> 00:31:09.370
So just with that, I just
want to say thank you

00:31:09.370 --> 00:31:10.480
for being here.

